WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:09.430
The China Huarong story is
about stress and turmoil at

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:14.430
what is one of China's largest
managers of distressed debt.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.860
In one of the country's most high-profile

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.130
corruption cases,

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.050
the former chairman of state-owned

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.763
China Huarong Asset Management
has been sentenced to death.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.407
Lai Xiaomin was convicted
of receiving more than

00:00:28.407 --> 00:00:31.970
$270 million over a decade.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.530
The rare and tough sentence...

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.630
That was considered a
particularly harsh punishment

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.170
for corruption.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.200
In part the swift
sentencing was taken to be

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.520
the latest sign of the
Chinese Communist Party

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.000
cracking down on corruption,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.010
particularly within the financial system

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.890
and within the political system.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.910
We do have breaking news
coming from Huarong,

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.250
this troubled asset manager
here. Just seeing reports

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.690
of a looming restructuring of
course playing out on this,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.650
and the bond rout there is deepening.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.960
Investors concerned about
the finances of state-owned

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.250
bad-debt manager China Huarong,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.290
and its silence isn't helping.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.750
I'm standing outside...

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.440
China Huarong's bond prices
have been incredibly volatile

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.350
over the last two months.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.060
It's of huge concern
because this could test

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.310
China's debt-ridden financial sectors

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.520
and the technocrats that run it

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.590
as well as all of the foreign banks

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.143
and the investors who have been
caught in between the drama.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.360
My name is Rebecca Choong Wilkins.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.430
I'm a reporter and
editor for Bloomberg News

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.923
China credit team.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.660
China Huarong is one of China's largest

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:54.800
distressed-debt managers.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.770
It's a so-called bad bank.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:00.790
It has long been considered
of systemic importance,

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.830
partly because its largest shareholder

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.563
is the Ministry of Finance.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.420
It was created in 1999 along
with three other asset managers

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.500
to try and deal with some of the bad debt

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.820
in China's economy.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.210
So those were things like bonds or loans

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.270
that companies had defaulted on.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.720
And they're considered
essential to the running

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.220
of China's $54 trillion financial market.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.010
So-called bad banks help carry that risk

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.230
on their own balance sheet and help curb

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.120
all of that bad debt and
remove it essentially

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.560
away from the rest of
the financial system.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.130
And that allows China's
other financial institutions

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.210
and its other banks to continue

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.520
with different kinds of banking services

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.023
that essentially help China's
economy to continue growing.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.970
Lai Xiaomin ran China
Huarong from 2012 until 2018.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.810
He was also sometimes
known as the God of Wealth,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.640
had a background in some of China's

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.560
top financial institutions.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.440
He had posts in the People's Bank of China

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.700
as well as China's Banking and Insurance

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.193
Regulatory Commission.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.970
Under Lai Xiaomin's
leadership at China Huarong,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.320
he expanded from the
firm's original mandate

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.450
dealing with bad debts into things like

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.830
securities trading and trusts.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.660
He also turned to the public debt markets

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.230
to try and fund some of that expansion.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.270
So under Lai Xiaomin, we
also saw a massive expansion

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.570
of the firms' bonds, outstanding bonds.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.760
And those types of debts
were sold to both mainland

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.090
onshore investors as well
as global investors overseas

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:57.880
in the form of dollar
bonds as well as yuan-

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.990
and Hong Kong dollar-denominated bonds.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.940
Lai Xiaomin was sometimes
criticized for just caring about

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.380
short-term profits and trying
to go way beyond the brief

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.140
of what traditionally a
bad bank like China Huarong

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.240
would be dealing with.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.150
He was actually charged with bribery

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.883
and taking illicit payments.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.560
In the course of the trial,
it came to light that

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.960
Lai Xiaomin had over the
years collected a spate

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.460
of luxury cars, of luxurious apartments

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.520
and expensive watches.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.520
He had an apartment that he
had renamed "the supermarket"

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.880
that was later found to have cabinets

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.860
that were full of cash.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.970
And a lot of this came to
light during the trial,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.630
some of which was televised

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:10.890
and became quite a public spectacle.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.563
He was executed earlier this year.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.720
The former board chairman
of one of China's

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.070
biggest asset-management
companies, Lai Xiaomin

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.640
was executed on Friday, authorities said.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.133
It was found...

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:32.110
So Wang Zhanfeng was appointed afterwards

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.400
to take over the running of China Huarong

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.310
and essentially to try and
get it back onto a safe

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.090
and steady footing.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.880
That included things like
shaping up its financial health

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.530
and pulling back from
some of the riskier areas

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.293
that Lai Xiaomin has expanded
into under his leadership.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.560
He was seen to be
certainly not as flamboyant

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.260
as his predecessor,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.100
but also brought in specifically
to try and introduce

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.950
a more serious and
professional management style

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.623
at China Huarong too.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.710
Concerns first flared at the
company at the end of March

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.710
when the firm failed to release
its 2020 annual reports.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.640
Fundamentally, what it
means is that investors

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.440
and we don't have a very
clear insight into precisely

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.750
how that cleanup has actually
gone over the last year.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:33.400
So investors suddenly
started to worry that

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:37.660
there could be some issues
with its financial health.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:42.040
Now that was then exacerbated
by several media reports

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.380
that there could be a potential
financial restructuring

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:46.480
at China Huarong.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.290
Well, obviously this has
been a saga now. Last week

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.810
in a speculation about a
possible debt restructuring

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.440
sent its overseas bonds
down to record lows.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.250
Got another ratings downgrade for Huarong.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:58.040
You've got to wonder
whether this is a little bit

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.140
of a delayed reaction.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.230
Well, as we've seen it
has been a rocky April for

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.250
Huarong bond investors.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.390
And we have seen those notes
continue to hit fresh lows

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.840
and in the wake of this
New York Times report...

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:13.260
China Huarong's bond prices
have been incredibly volatile

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.560
over the last two months.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.440
In fact, they've been far and
away the most volatile trading

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.290
that we've seen in the
offshore bond market.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.280
And they've actually weighed
on the broader bond market

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.410
as well.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:30.440
China Huarong is one
of the bigger borrowers

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.860
among Chinese firms.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.130
At the moment it has
about $40 billion worth

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:40.000
of outstanding bonds in the
onshore and offshore market.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.220
About $4 billion of
that needs to be repaid

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.830
or refinanced this year.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.380
When China Huarong first started selling

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.150
a lot of offshore debt,

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.500
it was considered by investors
to be a really safe bet.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.820
It was considered a quasi-sovereign.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.270
It had an investment-grade quality rating

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.910
and the debt itself was really popular

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.620
among many institutional investors

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.730
and many international investors.

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:11.610
Firms like BlackRock,
Allianz and Goldman Sachs

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:14.510
were all once buyers of the notes.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:17.970
Now the offshore investors
are in a little bit

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.963
of a tricky situation.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.050
One reason why investors
have become so worried

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.890
and so panicked about China
Huarong is the relative silence

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:35.890
since they said they would be
delaying their 2020 results.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:40.320
We've really only seen the
company and regulators come out

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.320
once or twice with public
comments to try and ease concerns.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:48.790
Regulators, for example,
have said that China Huarong

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:50.320
has ample liquidity

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.330
and that its operations
are running normally.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:56.750
Similarly, we've seen some
comments from the company itself

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.390
also trying to reassure investors
that its liquidity is good

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.300
and that its operations
are running normally.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.660
However, there generally
has been a kind of

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:12.060
lack of information around
precisely what's going to happen

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:12.893
at the company.

00:09:12.893 --> 00:09:16.090
What we do know is that
senior management has become

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.430
much more accustomed to
meeting with regulators

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.810
and authorities and having
last-minute meetings.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.760
And we also know that the
company is now providing

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.390
a weekly update on how its
liquidity conditions are going

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.763
and whether or not its
operations are running normally.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:39.250
In the short term, China
Huarong is expected

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.070
to meet its debt repayments.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:44.160
And that's partly because
Huarong has been said to come

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.860
to this agreement where state
banks will support meeting

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:51.590
its debt obligations that
come due through August.

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:55.120
On the other hand, however,
the longer-term concerns

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.953
about the fate of China Huarong persist.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:19.510
Ultimately investors now are
waiting to see whether or not

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:24.010
Beijing and authorities
step in to provide a kind of

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.940
guarantee or a kind of
bailout to China Huarong.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.940
I think the assumption generally is that

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:35.833
authorities do not want to
see a failure at Huarong,

00:10:35.833 --> 00:10:38.053
which roils the broader market.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.500
If we were to see fundamental

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.520
and painful restructuring
of China Huarong,

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:50.610
or if we were allowed to see
any kind of payment failure

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.010
at a firm like Huarong,
which is considered to be

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:57.950
of systemic importance,
which was backed by

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:00.340
central government institutions,

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:03.230
then that really would
change the way that investors

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.773
look at this market.

